Saskatoon, Saskatchewan – I can almost hear the groans before finishing this first sentence. Not lentils. Anything but lentils. Unfairly perceived by many as a tasteless mélange served up by overly enthusiastic vegetarians.
The poor lentil falls squarely into the misunderstood category of pulses (the edible seeds of legumes). At the recent Love Your Lentils event held by Canadian Lentils, the official mark of Saskatchewan Pulse Growers region, I learned a lot about the humble lentil.
For starters, they have an exception export status and are superstars in the nutritional category, plus there are a myriad of recipes available that run the gamut from starters to mains to even dessert!
Saskatchewan is the heart of the lentil growing region in Canada. Over one and half million tons was grown in 2011, with 98% destined for export to such countries as Bangladesh, Columbia, Western Europe and Pakistan – making Canada the largest exporter of lentils in the world.
Commercial production of lentils in Western Canada began in 1970 with approximately 1,500 acres (600 hectares). Production in Saskatchewan has now increased to over 2.4 million acres (960,000 hectares).
Whether green, red, yellow, black or brown, small, medium or large, split, coated or de-hulled, lentils are an excellent source of fibre, potassium and foliate. Chock full of protein and iron, gluten-free, low GI (glycemic index) as well as skimpy on fats and calories, an obvious super food.
Why so shy about the lentil? In Saskatoon after judging the Love Your Lentils Canada Challenge, engaging TV chef, author and lentil advocate, Michael Smith has a theory: ��There��s a certain unfamiliarity to lentils. Firstly, they don��t have their roots in our food culture. Sure, in some of the ethnic groups, but not in main stream Canada. Secondly, we��re missing a few generations of cooks right now. Many of us don��t come from a tradition of cooking anymore, so when we see things that are unfamiliar to us, we automatically think they��re exotic and therefore difficult – which is just not true."
Smiths notes ��they��re Canadian and we take enormous pride in our Canadian ingredients. There is a strong undercurrent in all of us that if it��s Canadian; we want a to be a part of it."
Lentils are easy to find and are one of the most user-friendly foods around. Canadian Lentils offers inspiration with their Lentils for Every Season booklet, which provides surprisingly unique, but simple recipes suggestions such as a peach and raspberry crumble, Maple-Espresso Baked Lentils, Strawberry Shortcakes or Pasta e Fagioli to name a few. A copy of this nifty recipe guide is available at lentils.ca. So no more excuses, learn to love the lentil!
Recipes created by Chef Michael Smith for Canadian Lentils.
Lentil Puree
You may make your own lentil puree by simply pureeing four parts cooked lentils with one part water. It may also be stirred into any soup or stew.
Sneaky Lentil Cookies
These awesome cookies will impress everyone at your table. The kids will not know they have a secret ingredient, they will just know they taste good. But you will know they are packed with healthy lentil puree.
Ingredients:
1/2 cup (125 ml) butter, softened
2 cups (500 ml) brown sugar, packed
1 egg
2 tsp. (10 ml) vanilla
1/2 cup (125 ml) lentil puree
