Link Education Minister James Merlino. Photo: Chris Hopkins
The move follows a Melbourne principal resigning from his job after his decision to expel a student was overturned by the Department of Education.
He's one of many principals who say the current expulsion policy prioritises the rights of some children over the safety of others.
Advertisement
The girl he tried to expel threatened another student with a knife from the school's food technology room and repeatedly bullied a student with an intellectual disability.
The principal told The Age he quit his job because he could not guarantee the safety of his students.
"It's not good if you can't maintain the safety of the kids," he said.
"They have made it near impossible to expel a kid. I'm not going to be the principal of a school where a student gets stabbed."
The expulsion was appealed on the grounds that the school had provided inadequate support to the child.
But the principal said the school offered the student sessions with a psychologist and created a mental health plan.
He said the department's decision sent a concerning message to other students at the school.
"It sends a message that they can get away with bad behaviour," he said.
He said it had become more difficult to expel students following a damning Ombudsman's report last year.
Victorian Education Minister James Merlino said he was considering changes to expulsion processes and would have more to say in due course.
