Business is booming in information technology — so much so that the field is outpacing the number of qualified workers in terms of growth.
As part of its mission to provide a skilled workforce, Ivy Tech Community College is meeting that demand head-on by expanding offerings in its School of Information Technology.
"The program is growing. We had to add sections," Department Chair Ben Marrero said. "There's a lack of skilled employees in the workforce. Employers have the jobs but can't find skilled workers. Information technology is one of the fields where people can make themselves extremely employable."
Ivy Tech has undertaken a number of initiatives to help fill that gap, including adding a data center at its Gary-35th Avenue location (one previously opened at its Valparaiso campus more than a year ago) and expanding courses in its cybersecurity program.
The college also offers a free certification that will qualify graduates to work in computer repair or tech support.
"We are trying to help people become employable," Marrero said. "There is a big need for computer skills. Cybersecurity is a hot field. We get calls all the time from people looking for employees."
Through its Workforce Alignment arm, Ivy Tech also provides computer network training to companies, and to dual credit instructors so they can teach those courses at their high schools.
The IT program is so good that a team of students from the Valparaiso campus recently placed second nationwide in the National Collegiate Cyber Defense Competition, a matchup between top cybersecurity teams from higher ed institutions across the nation. The goal of the competition is to see who can penetrate other teams' computer networks in a "capture the flag" format.
Teams also receive points based on their ability to detect and respond to outside threats, maintain availability of existing services such as email and webservers, respond to business requests such as the addition or removal of services and balance security needs against business needs. This is just one of several competitions students attend annually.
Ivy Tech also plans to add a Visual Communications program, focused on graphics programming and creating video games.
IT programs are available at all Ivy Tech locations in Northwest Indiana, although some courses may be online only.
"All our faculty are highly qualified. They either have certifications or have been doing this type of work for three to five years," Marrero said.
Michael Persin, of Hobart, who graduated in May with an associate's degree in Network Infrastructure, has been involved with computers since he was 8.
"This program was right up my alley," said Persin, who wants to work as a network administrator and start his own entertainment business. "It's quite an interesting field."
