It's never been so unanimous that the coordinator of the No. 128-ranked offense in the nation has to return next season.
But will he?
When he was hired as Rutgers offensive coordinator in December, Jerry Kill made it clear that he wanted to end the revolving door at his position. The Scarlet Knights have changed offensive play-callers after each of the last eight seasons -- an unparalleled streak around the nation.
"Yeah, at this point in time," Kill said Monday when asked by NJ Advance Media if he still plans to return in 2018. "As long as the health issues don't prevail, I'll be here. I don't have any plans at this point in time. That (health) I can't control, but so far, so good."
B1G bowl projections: Ohio State gets pushed out?
It's extremely unlikely that Kill -- who signed a three-year contract as the highest-paid assistant coach in Rutgers history -- would leave for another job, as was the case last season when Drew Mehringer's one-year run ended with him becoming pass game coordinator at Texas.
And Rutgers coach Chris Ash made it clear that he wants Kill to return in spite of the statistical ranks. Rutgers has three Big Ten wins in part because of Kill's change to a run-based ball-control offense has allowed for complementary football with the defense and special teams.
The real X-factor is Kill's health. The 56-year-old former Big Ten Coach of the Year at Minnesota suffers from epilepsy that forced him to retire from coaching in October 2015 and caused him to miss two days of work earlier this season after he suffered a seizure in a team meeting room.
Kill certainly isn't the first Rutgers offensive coordinator in the last decade to pay lip service to his intentions to return before the season ended.
It's also common practice for coaches around the country to leave a backdoor out on such remarks by saying that discussions of the future do not happen until after the season.
But Monday marked Kill's final scheduled appearance before the Rutgers media until spring practice in March. For those looking to read between the lines, Kill was willing to look ahead to next season's roster makeup, which could be a hint that he truly favors returning.
"Coach Kill is a great coach," said tight end Jerome Washington, Rutgers' leading receiver. "He's very intelligent. He works relentless hours that people wouldn't see unless you are in the building. He puts his heart and soul into everything. That's the person he is."
Kill said before the season that the keys to his health included sleep, exercise and a healthy diet. He tweaked his weekly routine after the early-season seizure to include walks around the stadium concourse.
Kill also serves as the quarterbacks coach. Rutgers started Kyle Bolin for the first five games, Gio Rescigno for the last six and a vocal portion of the fan base is calling for true freshman Johnathan Lewis to start the season finale against Michigan State.
