With an ease and grace that are almost intoxicating, Olivier Assayas' "Les Destinees"--based on the 20th Century classic French novel by Jacques Chardonne--sweeps us into the past (1900-30) and into a world of elegant chateaux, candelabra-lit ballrooms, bittersweet loves and buoyant waltzes, business battles, long marriages, scandals, the horrors of war and a final fragile acceptance of life's unavoidable pain and its inescapable passage to death.
This is a novel adaptation on the grand scale of movies like "Gone With the Wind," "The Godfather" or Viscontis "The Leopard": one of those long, densely detailed, highly evocative films that track the fortunes and follies of a large family through decades of change and evolution. And, in tackling this kind of subject--and this particular novel--Assayas reveals himself as a filmmaker of unexpected breadth and reach. We know him up to now mainly as a specialist in films about youth and contemporary society, such as "Cold Water" and "Irma Vep."
Here, this progressive artist becomes a past master, bringing to life the plenitude and vicissitudes of a French upper-class community before World War II. His subjects, all of whom live in fictitious "Barbizac," are the Barnery family, who own a high-endporcelain factory, and the Pommerels, who bottle and sell fine cognac. The clans' main representatives are a married couple, Jean and Pauline Barnery (Charles Berling and Emmanuelle Beart), who meet and fall in love in their youth, marry after some difficulty and settle into a life of seeming bounty but actual slow deterioration and loss.
"Les Destinees" is divided into three acts, the first of which follows Jean's early life as a local minister--during which he rejects his first wife, Nathalie (Isabelle Huppert), after wrongly suspecting her of adultery, then meets the young Pauline and later, in remorse over his error about Nathalie, transfers his inheritance and leaves Barbizac. The second captures the blissful idyll of the now-married Jean and Pauline in the Swiss mountains, where they live temporarily, as artist-recluses, only for each other. Act 3 sees Jean seize the reins of his family business when his accountant brother Frederic (Jean-Baptiste Malartre) proves inept, and settle into a world of porcelain-making and finance--chained to the increasingly foundering fortunes of his high-quality product--with a brief, bloody interlude in WWI.
By the time "Les Destinees" ends, we feel we have seen and understood these two lives with unusual fullness, as well as a love that believably binds Jean and Pauline up to their very last moment together.
The movie is realized with the same loving care Jean lavishes on his porcelain services: not for the mass market, but, with consummate affection and skill, for the cognoscenti. The sets are richly decorated, the compositions and lighting (by cinematographer Eric Gautier) irresistibly recall the paintings of Bonnard and Vuillard, the camera moving like a whirligig through smudged glowing interiors.
The huge cast is almost uniformly impressive--even when, like Remi Martin's cad, their roles approach melodrama--and Beart and Berling both give great performances. Berling's sternly maintained sobriety and inner anguish make him magnetic, and Beart--who is really the beating heart of the picture--is warmly emotional and enchantingly beautiful. Like Vivien Leigh in "Gone With the Wind," or Elizabeth Taylor in "Giant," Beart's dazzling good looks become the film's dramatic touchstone.
"Les Destinees" is a novel highly sympathetic to France's old ruling class from a novelist whose collaborationist politics left him in disgrace after World War II. That's all the more reason it's such a departure for the leftist Assayas. But Chardonne gave us this marvelously detailed picture because of his background, deep knowledge and sympathies; his father was a cognac merchant and his mother's family, the Havilands, made fine porcelain. ("Barbizac" is a fictional composite of the cities Barbezieux and Jarnac.) Assayas makes that vision his own and then enhances it, with the heightened perspective of someone from a later generation who loves Chardonne's work while condemning his wartime activities: his collaborationist writings for Drieu La Rochelle. Just as the novels of Knut Hamsun or Louis-Ferdinand Celine redeem their authors' lamentable World War II politics through their literary genius, "Les Destinees" transcends Chardonne's failings.
Though far different from anything Assayas has made before, "Les Destinees" shows him at his best. Remember that you're watching a complex, three-hour film that will show you, in great detail, two very intricate lives--but is always enlivened by Assayas' sensibility, Berling's charisma and Beart's rapturous beauty--and you won't begrudge an instant.
