The cast of Beyond Bollywood have said that the production will showcase the talent and the culture of India.
Pooja Pant, who portrays a world-renowned Kathak dancer in the show, said that audiences will experience a range of Indian dance styles.
She told Digital Spy: "It's wonderful to be part of such a production where all different types of dancers are coming together. It's very special to be a part of such a production where there's so much variety, from contemporary, Kathak and Bollywood. So there's a coming together of dance styles that is very exciting.
Advertisement - Continue Reading Below
"I think the audience will enjoy it because of the variety and each style has its own uniqueness to show. It's very enjoyable and there won't be any issues of whether you will understand it [and] whether you know the language or not."
Sudeep Modak, a star of Marathi TV dramas, who plays dual roles of the central character Shailey Shergill's father and the hero Raghav's sidekick Ballu, revealed that his characters bring out both the conflict and the fun in the show.
"The father is the main antagonist of this play. He is the one that causes all the pain to the mother in the story and it's his constant pressure over Shailey and the mother that comes across as monstrous. He doesn't intend to be so bad but its stems from his complete lack of understanding of what an artist is.
"Ballu by comparison is out and out mischievous. He's the humour element in the play. He's this funny chap who enjoys every moment to the fullest. He supports everything that Raghav does and is on this journey with him and Shailey."
Describing the challenges of portraying two characters in the production, he said: "I'm probably the only non-dancer in the show and I'm probably the only actor-actor not a dancer actor. Rajeev needed an actor who can do multiple roles. He realised it would be a nice challenge to me as an actor to play a funny part and a villainous character. I was also very excited by that opportunity."
Describing the objectives of the ambitious production, Modak explained: "The intention is to bring the Indian elements and culture to it and take the true Indian dance forms to the UK that is the primary intention of the story but there are also these elements in the narrative that will make it relatable to an NRI audience."
Advertisement - Continue Reading Below
British choreographer Jackie Biggs has been enlisted to support the Indian dancers and prepare them for their West End debut. A professional dancer with over 16 years experience in productions such as Dirty Dancing, Biggs described the experience of working in India with the cast and crew.
"It's a very different way of working. They don't really use counts that much. They learn the music, they sing every song and learn every song off by heart, whereas in the UK and US we just count the rhythm so you can run a whole number without the music. So its been really interesting to swap the way I work.
